  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given two arrays AAA and BBB, of lengths NNN and MMM respectively. You are also given an empty array CCC.\nIn one operation you can:\n\n\nAppend the entirety of AAA to CCC; or\nIncrease any element of CCC by 111.\nFind the minimum number of operations needed such that BBB becomes a subsequence of CCC or print −1-1−1 if it is impossible to make BBB as a subsequence of CCC after some number of operations.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of three lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM — the lengths of arrays AAA and BBB.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1A_1A1​,A2A_2A2​,…,AN\\ldots , A_N…,AN​ — the elements of array AAA.\nThe third line contains MMM space-separated integers B1B_1B1​,B2B_2B2​,…,BM\\ldots ,B_M…,BM​ — the elements of array BBB.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum number of operations needed such that BBB becomes a subsequence of CCC or print −1-1−1 if it is impossible to make BBB as a subsequence of CCC after some number of operations.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤M≤2⋅1051 \\leq M \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤M≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai,Bi≤1091 \\leq A_i, B_i \\leq 10^91≤Ai​,Bi​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105.",
      "The sum of MMM over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n2 3\n5 2\n4 5 3\n1 4\n10\n11 15 14 10\n5 3\n3 9 10 5 5\n8 1 2\n4 3\n1 3 1 2\n1 1 2",
        "output": "5\n14\n-1\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: One sequence of operations is as follows.\n\nInitially, C=[]C = []C=[]. Append AAA to it, to obtain [5,2][5, 2][5,2].\nAppend AAA to it again, to obtain C=[5,2,5,2]C = [5, 2 ,5 ,2]C=[5,2,5,2].\nIncrease C2C_2C2​ twice and C4C_4C4​ once, to obtain [5,4,5,3][5, 4, 5, 3][5,4,5,3].\nThis has B=[4,5,3]B = [4, 5, 3]B=[4,5,3] as a subsequence, as required. We used 555 moves in total.\n\nTest case 222: Append AAA to CCC four times to obtain [10,10,10,10][10, 10, 10, 10][10,10,10,10], then increase the elements to obtain C=[11,15,14,10]C = [11, 15, 14, 10]C=[11,15,14,10]. This requires 141414 moves in total.\nTest case 333: It can be shown that no matter what, BBB cannot appear as a subsequence of CCC.\nTest case 444: Append AAA to CCC once. BBB is already a subsequence of AAA, so this is enough."
      }
    ]
